From ee2d4e1a8759296b7e52a0a15d2f632eb9114b31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 07:12:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(particles): route server effects through native playback

Decode packet 118 as one reusable model that retains its unsigned
dimension, signed unique actor ID, float position, effect name, and
optional Molang JSON without rewriting typed nested values.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 uses -1 for world-space effects. Its
client handler 14133fb50 drops effects from another dimension, ignores
missing attached actors, and passes attached positions to the bound
factory as offsets. Resolve standard Java particles from the tracked
actor's Java feet origin, then add the offset with float arithmetic.
This gives both direct and ViaProxy ordinary clients an initial
position; Java particle packets cannot retain an actor attachment.

The target's serializer 143aa8310 still writes a boolean before the
Molang JSON string. Versioned protocol schema metadata describes the
field differently, so preserve the executable's encoding. Native value
serialization 1421f9380/1421f9f70 uses an array of named typed values,
including recursive member_array objects. The model preserves those
bytes for native playback integration; the Java fallback cannot
interpret arbitrary custom graphs or Molang variables.

Verification executes 100 native serialization cases and 1600 native
handler admission cases with supplied stream, lookup, context-copy and
factory boundaries. Unit tests cover native field order, signed ID
limits, optional-field boundaries, nested JSON retention, missing
actors, and float-coordinate precision. Resource transport, native
emitter integration, interpolation and visible parity remain pending.

Transport complete particle requests to clients advertising native playback,
including actor UID, matching Java ID/UUID, raw Molang JSON and the resolved
initial position. Preserve the fully encoded Java particle body for client
fallback. Clients without the channel retain the existing Java translation.

Parse typed variables in core without flattening member arrays. Keep names,
ordered duplicate members and the native JSON float conversions. Only root
assignment sanitizes non-finite scalars. Native executable comparisons cover
2,042 scalar conversion cases and 200 first-member/case-sensitive nested
lookups. Keep malformed documents bounded. Client playback, actor lifetime,
and visible particle comparisons must still establish their own behavior.

Native JSON reader 14e0334a0/14e0369a0 accepts comments, raw string controls,
leading integer zeros and trailing data after its first document. Normalize
these observed forms before strict decoding so valid packet variables do
not silently select Java fallback. Keep string/comment boundaries in the
size and nesting guards. Match the native high-surrogate continuation rule
without enabling unrelated lenient syntax. Fifty-four executable probes
verify reader acceptance and array admission with private allocation and
byte-copy boundaries. Additional numeric grammar, wire UTF-8 decoding,
packet-error propagation and visible playback remain unverified.

Execute HashedString constructor 1406acb20 for 1,008 supplied UTF-8 names.
It uses FNV-1, multiplying before XOR, and assigns zero to empty names.
Encode native JsonCpp surrogate bytes explicitly instead of replacing them
with Java's UTF-8 replacement character. Four native decoded strings verify
high-surrogate continuation and lone low-surrogate bytes. Keep C-string
termination and case-sensitive names, with valid script casing handled by
client bindings. Direct and ViaProxy share the same core hash implementation.
